        {
          "type": "practice",
          "title": "Practice: Read an ENSO Forecast",
          "content": "**A real NOAA forecast in May says**: \"Niño 3.4 index +1.4°C and rising. Forecast: strong El Niño through Northern Hemisphere winter, 75% probability.\"\n\n**What should each region expect for next winter (Dec–Feb)?**\n\n1. **Southern California**: \n   → Above-average rainfall (subtropical jet shifts south, atmospheric rivers more frequent).\n\n2. **Pacific Northwest**:\n   → Drier and milder than normal — storm track displaced south.\n\n3. **Northern Tier (Minnesota, Dakotas)**:\n   → Milder winter — polar jet retreats north.\n\n4. **Gulf Coast (Texas to Florida)**:\n   → Wetter than normal — subtropical jet active.\n\n5. **Australia**:\n   → Drier than normal — warm pool spread east means less moisture for them.\n\n6. **Peru**:\n   → Wetter than normal (catastrophic floods possible). Fishery collapse.\n\n7. **Global temperature**:\n   → Likely a record-warm year — El Niño adds short-term heat on top of long-term warming.\n\n**Planning implications**:\n• California water managers: prepare reservoirs\n• Australian wheat farmers: drought planning\n• US Energy: lower heating demand in north\n• Public health: malaria & dengue zones shift",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">PRACTICE: ENSO READ</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Niño 3.4 +1.4°C, strong El Niño</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">S. California: wet</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"265\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Subtropical jet shifts south</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"320\" width=\"940\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"365\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">Pacific NW: dry</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"405\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Storm track displaced south</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"460\" width=\"940\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"505\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">N. Tier US: mild winter</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"545\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Polar jet retreats north</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"600\" width=\"940\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(20,184,166,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"645\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">Gulf Coast: wet</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"685\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Active subtropical jet</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"740\" width=\"940\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"785\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">Australia: drought</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"825\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Warm pool migrated east</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"880\" width=\"940\" height=\"120\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"925\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">Global: likely record warm</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"965\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">El Niño + long-term trend</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Translating ENSO forecast to regional impacts"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "During a strong El Niño, why does the Atlantic hurricane season typically become quieter while Pacific tropical activity increases?",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "El Niño weakens Coriolis force in the Atlantic.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "El Niño increases upper-level wind shear over the Atlantic, tearing storms apart, while reducing shear and warming SSTs in the Eastern Pacific.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "El Niño cools the Atlantic enough to prevent hurricanes.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "El Niño shifts the equator northward.",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "explanation": "El Niño strengthens the **subtropical jet stream** over the Atlantic, producing strong **upper-level westerly winds** that shear the tops off developing tropical storms. Meanwhile, the Eastern Pacific warm pool **expands**, with lower shear — favoring more frequent and intense storms there. La Niña does the opposite: weak Atlantic shear + warm Atlantic = active hurricane season (2020 La Niña: 30 named storms, exhausting the alphabet).",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">KNOWLEDGE CHECK</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">ENSO and hurricanes</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"200\" width=\"500\" height=\"320\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">EL NIÑO</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Atlantic: quiet</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">High wind shear tears storms</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"395\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Pacific: enhanced</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"430\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Warm + low shear</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"475\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Subtropical jet active</text><rect x=\"540\" y=\"200\" width=\"500\" height=\"320\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"790\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"24\">LA NIÑA</text><text x=\"790\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Atlantic: HYPERACTIVE</text><text x=\"790\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Low shear, warm water</text><text x=\"790\" y=\"395\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Pacific: quieter</text><text x=\"790\" y=\"430\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Cool tongue extends</text><text x=\"790\" y=\"475\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">2020: 30 named storms</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"560\" width=\"940\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"620\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">SHEAR IS KING</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"670\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Upper-level shear destroys storms</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"710\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Hurricanes need vertical alignment</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"820\" width=\"940\" height=\"200\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"880\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">SEASONAL FORECAST</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"925\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">NOAA uses ENSO state to predict</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"965\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Atlantic hurricane outlook</text></svg>",
            "caption": "ENSO controls hurricane basins"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Marine Heatwaves and the Future Ocean",
          "content": "**Marine heatwaves (MHW)** — periods of anomalously warm ocean — have **doubled** in frequency since 1980.\n\n**Recent examples**:\n• **The Blob** (NE Pacific, 2013–16): SSTs +3°C, killed seabirds, sea lions, salmon runs\n• **Mediterranean 2022**: +5°C anomaly, mass die-off of seagrass, fish, octopi\n• **North Atlantic 2023**: hottest ocean temperatures ever recorded — June anomalies broke instrumental records by an unprecedented margin\n\n**Causes**:\n• Long-term ocean warming (background heat-up)\n• Reduced mixing as warm fresh surface layers stratify\n• Atmospheric blocking high-pressure systems that trap heat\n\n**Ecosystem impacts**:\n• **Mass coral bleaching** — corals expel symbiotic algae under heat stress. 2024 was the **fourth global mass bleaching event** in 30 years.\n• **Species poleward shifts** — fish, krill, plankton migrating ~70 km/decade\n• **Fishery collapses** — Pacific cod, herring, sardine\n• **Mass mortality** in invertebrates that can't move\n\n**The future ocean**: warmer, more stratified, more acidic, with poleward-shifting ecosystems, smaller and less diverse fish populations. Already underway.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">MARINE HEATWAVES</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Doubled since 1980</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"170\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">The Blob (2013-16)</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"275\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">NE Pacific +3°C, salmon dead</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Years-long event</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"370\" width=\"940\" height=\"170\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">Mediterranean 2022</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"465\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">+5°C, mass invertebrate die-off</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"500\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Octopi, seagrass devastated</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"560\" width=\"940\" height=\"170\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"610\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">N. Atlantic 2023</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"655\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">Hottest ever — by huge margin</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"690\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Shocked oceanographers</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"750\" width=\"940\" height=\"170\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(20,184,166,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"800\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"22\">4th global coral bleach (2024)</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"845\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"20\">In 30 years</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"880\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Reefs collapsing</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"980\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"22\">Ocean is shifting under our feet</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Ocean heat extremes are the new normal"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "application",
          "title": "Connect the Sea to Your Life",
          "content": "Three things you can do tomorrow to engage with ocean science:\n\n1. **Track an ENSO forecast.** NOAA's Climate Prediction Center publishes ENSO Diagnostic Discussions monthly. Read one. You will instantly know more than 99% of citizens about why next winter might be wet or dry where you live.\n\n2. **Calculate your seafood's stress.** Pull up the **Seafood Watch** (Monterey Bay Aquarium) guide before your next sushi or seafood purchase. Acidification + warming + overfishing = some species are tanking. Your choices vote with money.\n\n3. **Look at a sea-level rise map.** NOAA's Sea Level Rise Viewer lets you toggle scenarios for any U.S. coast. Look at your hometown — or a place you love — under 1m, 2m, 3m of rise. If it shapes a real-estate or career decision, even better.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">YOUR PRACTICE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Three ocean-literacy actions</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"200\" width=\"900\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">1. Read ENSO forecast</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">NOAA Climate Prediction Center</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"345\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Know next winter before everyone</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"450\" width=\"900\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">2. Use Seafood Watch</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"560\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Monterey Bay Aquarium guide</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"595\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Vote with your fork</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"640\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Direct lever on fisheries</text><rect x=\"100\" y=\"700\" width=\"900\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"760\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">3. NOAA SLR viewer</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"810\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Toggle 1m, 2m, 3m scenarios</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"845\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Inform real-estate decisions</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"890\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Coastal future made visible</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Three ocean-literacy habits"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "Surface ocean has absorbed about 30% of human CO₂ emissions. This dissolves to form carbonic acid. By how much has surface ocean H⁺ concentration changed since pre-industrial times?",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "Decreased by ~30%.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Roughly unchanged — ocean buffers any CO₂.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Increased by ~30%.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Decreased by 10× because of biological pump.",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "explanation": "Ocean pH has dropped from ~8.2 to ~8.05–8.1 — a change of about **0.15 pH units**. Because **pH is a logarithmic scale**, that small-looking drop equals a **~30% increase** in hydrogen ion concentration. Calcium-carbonate-shell organisms are already affected. The misconception of \"buffering\" is partly true — ocean alkalinity does buffer chemistry — but not fast enough to keep pace with current CO₂ emission rates.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">KNOWLEDGE CHECK</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Acidification math</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"200\" width=\"940\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">pH DROP</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">8.2 → ~8.05</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">−0.15 pH units</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"395\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Looks small...</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"450\" width=\"940\" height=\"220\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">H⁺ CHANGE</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"560\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">+30% H⁺ concentration</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"600\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">(logarithmic scale)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"645\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">...but acidity is logarithmic</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"700\" width=\"940\" height=\"320\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"755\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">CONSEQUENCES</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"805\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Shell-builders struggle</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"845\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Pteropods already dissolving</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"890\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Coral reefs collapsing</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"940\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Logarithmic scales fool us</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Why ocean acidification matters"
          }
        }
