          "type": "quiz",
          "question": "(Reviewing L3-L5) What is the fundamental difference between weather and climate?",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "Weather and climate are the same thing at different sizes.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Weather is the instantaneous state of the atmosphere; climate is the statistical distribution of weather over ~30 years.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Weather is short-term cycles; climate is long-term cycles caused only by the sun.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Climate is what's predicted; weather is what's measured.",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "explanation": "From Lesson 5: **weather** is what's happening right now — temperature, wind, precipitation at a particular place and time. **Climate** is the long-run statistical distribution: 30-year averages and the typical variability around them. \"A cold day\" is weather; \"the cold season\" is climate. Crucially, weather is **chaotic and unpredictable beyond ~2 weeks**, but climate is **statistically predictable** because it depends on stable boundary conditions like solar input, atmospheric composition, and ocean heat content. This is why \"it snowed yesterday\" doesn't refute climate change — it's a weather event nested inside a shifting climate distribution. Lessons 3-4 grounded this in the dynamics: atmospheric circulation (Hadley/Ferrel/Polar cells), ocean currents, the water cycle.",
