          "explanation": "From Lessons 9-10: **global mean temperature is a heavily-averaged number** — most of the local change is much larger (Arctic warming is 3-4× the global average). The last glacial maximum, 20,000 years ago, was only **about 5°C colder** than today, with ice sheets covering Chicago and sea level ~120 m lower. Going +2°C is therefore about **40% of an ice-age-magnitude change in the opposite direction**, on a timescale of decades rather than millennia. The impacts cascade: ice-sheet loss, sea-level rise, ocean acidification, heatwaves, drought intensification, agricultural disruption, biodiversity loss, and tipping points (Amazon dieback, AMOC slowdown, permafrost thaw). \"2°C\" sounds small because it's deeply averaged; the physical consequences are anything but.",
